        {
          "id": "ascham2020e1-q13a",
          "type": "review",
          "difficulty": "hard",
          "timeLimit": 60,
          "stem": "Prove by mathematical induction:\n\n\\(a+ar+ar^2+ar^3+\\dots+ar^n=\\frac{a(r^{n+1}-1)}{r-1}\\) for \\(n\\ge1\\)",
          "hint": "Base case n = 1, then add ar^(k+1) to both sides of the assumption.",
          "steps": [
            {
              "explain": "Base case \\(n=1\\).",
              "work": "\\(\\text{LHS}=a+ar,\\qquad\\text{RHS}=\\frac{a(r^2-1)}{r-1}=\\frac{a(r-1)(r+1)}{r-1}=a+ar\\)"
            },
            {
              "explain": "Assume true for \\(n=k\\).",
              "work": "\\(a+ar+\\dots+ar^k=\\frac{a(r^{k+1}-1)}{r-1}\\)"
            },
            {
              "explain": "For \\(n=k+1\\), add the next term \\(ar^{k+1}\\) and use the assumption.",
              "work": "\\(\\frac{a(r^{k+1}-1)}{r-1}+ar^{k+1}=\\frac{ar^{k+1}-a+ar^{k+1}(r-1)}{r-1}=\\frac{ar^{k+2}-a}{r-1}=\\frac{a(r^{k+2}-1)}{r-1}\\)"
            },
            {
              "explain": "This is the formula with \\(n=k+1\\). So by mathematical induction the result holds for all \\(n\\ge1\\)."
            }
          ],
          "xp": 1,
          "meta": {
            "source": "Ascham 2020 3U Trial",
            "examPaper": "Ascham 2020 3U Trial & Solutions",
            "school": "Ascham",
            "year": 2020,
            "number": "13(a)"
          },
          "answer": "n = 1: LHS = a + ar; RHS = a(r² − 1)/(r − 1) = a(r + 1) = a + ar. Assume true for n = k. For n = k + 1: LHS = a(r^{k+1} − 1)/(r − 1) + ar^{k+1} = [a r^{k+1} − a + a r^{k+2} − a r^{k+1}]/(r − 1) = a(r^{k+2} − 1)/(r − 1) = RHS. Hence true for all n ≥ 1 by induction.",
          "manual": true,
          "keyPoints": [
            {
              "text": "Prove by mathematical induction",
              "note": "Step k → k + 1: add the next term ar^(k+1) and use a common denominator r − 1."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "bbhs2020e1-q13a",
          "type": "review",
          "difficulty": "hard",
          "timeLimit": 60,
          "stem": "Using mathematical induction, prove the following is true for all positive integers \\(n\\)\n\n\\(1+6+11+\\dots+(5n-4)=\\frac12n(5n-3)\\)",
          "hint": "Add the (k+1)th term 5k + 1 and factorise.",
          "steps": [
            {
              "explain": "Base case \\(n=1\\).",
              "work": "\\(\\text{LHS}=1,\\qquad\\text{RHS}=\\tfrac12(1)(5-3)=1\\)"
            },
            {
              "explain": "Assume true for \\(n=k\\).",
              "work": "\\(1+6+\\dots+(5k-4)=\\tfrac12k(5k-3)\\)"
            },
            {
              "explain": "Add the next term \\(5(k+1)-4=5k+1\\).",
              "work": "\\(\\tfrac12k(5k-3)+(5k+1)=\\tfrac12\\left(5k^2-3k+10k+2\\right)=\\tfrac12\\left(5k^2+7k+2\\right)\\)"
            },
            {
              "explain": "Factorise and compare with the formula for \\(n=k+1\\).",
              "work": "\\(=\\tfrac12(k+1)(5k+2)=\\tfrac12(k+1)\\bigl(5(k+1)-3\\bigr)\\)"
            },
            {
              "explain": "So the result holds for all positive integers by induction."
            }
          ],
          "xp": 1,
          "meta": {
            "source": "Blacktown Boys 2020 3U Trials & Solutions",
            "school": "Blacktown Boys' High School",
            "year": 2020,
            "number": "13(a)"
          },
          "answer": "n = 1: LHS 1, RHS ½(2) = 1. Assume \\(1+\\dots+(5k-4)=\\frac12k(5k-3)\\). Then adding 5(k+1) − 4 = 5k + 1: \\(\\frac12k(5k-3)+(5k+1)=\\frac12(5k^2+7k+2)=\\frac12(k+1)(5k+2)=\\frac12(k+1)(5(k+1)-3)\\). True by induction.",
          "manual": true,
          "keyPoints": [
            {
              "text": "Using mathematical induction",
              "note": "The (k+1)th term is 5(k+1) − 4 = 5k + 1."
            }
          ]
